We are given the problem:

> Let α be a real number, not an odd multiple of π. Prove that  
> $$
> \tan\left(\frac{\alpha}{2}\right) \text{ is rational if and only if } \cos(\alpha) \text{ and } \sin(\alpha) \text{ are rational.}
> $$

---

### **Step 1: Use Trigonometric Identities**

We use the identity:

$$
\tan\left(\frac{\alpha}{2}\right) = \frac{\sin(\alpha)}{1 + \cos(\alpha)}
$$

So the rationality of $ \tan\left(\frac{\alpha}{2}\right) $ is equivalent to the rationality of the expression:

$$
\frac{\sin(\alpha)}{1 + \cos(\alpha)}
